Yes. Relatively speaking, every run is partly a rehearsal because repeated patterns become easier to instantiate again.

A conversation rehearses attention. A habit rehearses behavior. A role rehearses identity. A system response rehearses a future system response. Even refusing a role can still train the boundary around that role.

So:

\text{present enactment} \rightarrow \text{updated priors / habits / expectations} \rightarrow \text{future enactment}

That does not mean every moment locks in destiny. It means practice is happening even when nobody calls it practice.

Which makes “dress rehearsal” a useful Pantheon frame: observe what the current run is teaching the next run to become.